--
-- step-1.sequences.sql: sets the sequence of every identity column step 1 moves into Aurora
-- PostgreSQL, for job job1 (assignment v2). GENERATED by scripts/render_dms_step.py from step
-- 1's plan and transformation contract; regenerate it rather than editing it.
--
-- YOUR RESPONSIBILITY: Creating, configuring, securing and running this Aurora cluster, and
-- running the DDL in step-1.ddl.sql, are your responsibility. This is a suggested starting
-- point generated from your assessment, not a production-ready configuration: review every
-- setting against your own requirements, policies and backups before you deploy it. The data
-- movement stack (dms-step-1.yaml) needs the settings listed here; nothing we deploy changes
-- the cluster's configuration or runs inside it, and the DMS task only connects as the DMS user
-- you create to write the rows.
--
-- Why: DMS writes each row's id as the source has it but never advances the column's sequence,
-- so after the load every sequence still starts at 1 and the first insert without an explicit
-- id collides with a loaded row. The gate compares rows only and cannot see this.
--
-- When to run it: at cutover, after your applications stopped writing to the source and DMS
-- applied the last change, and before anything writes to Aurora. Each statement sets the
-- sequence so the next value is the column's maximum plus one; running it again is safe.
-- How: psql "host=<writer endpoint> dbname=shop user=<admin user> sslmode=verify-full
-- sslrootcert=<RDS CA bundle>" -f step-1.sequences.sql
--
-- Identity columns: 2.

SELECT setval(pg_get_serial_sequence('"public"."customers"', 'id'), COALESCE(MAX("id"), 0) + 1, false) FROM "public"."customers";
SELECT setval(pg_get_serial_sequence('"public"."orders"', 'id'), COALESCE(MAX("id"), 0) + 1, false) FROM "public"."orders";
